{
 "about": "The models MapNG places for an OSM tag, chosen from data/game-model-catalogue.json: per tag, per region of the world (any, north-america, europe, tropical), in order of preference; the first is the usual one, the rest are variety. A pick's key is the tag, or the tag and a variant after # (highway=bus_stop#shelter: the model for a stop with a shelter). And what is known of a model beyond the catalogue's own record: yaw, the degrees added to the direction it should face so its front looks that way (a bench's seat towards the road): set here only where it differs from, or adds to, the front the catalogue records for the model (+x: 0, +y: -90, -x: 180, -y: 90), and seen, the day it was looked at in the running game. scripts/tag-library/from-catalogue.mjs checks this file against the catalogue and writes data/tag-models.json, which the export reads (services/tagModels.js). objects: the mapped points the export places a picked model at, each a rule: tag (the OSM tag of the point), when / unless (other tags it must or must not have, a list of values each), pick (the key in picks), face (road: its front to the nearest road; along: its long side along the road; none), item (the What to build switch it belongs to), cap (the most placed in one level). parts: the models the code builds with by name (a street light for a class of road, a gate, a rail and its post, a sign): each the tag it stands for in the library (or untagged:<name>), its shape, and facts, what the code needs to know of that model (where its lamp is, how long a piece runs). Read through services/tagModels.js part(). A part with ship: true has its materials sent with every export (models from one level's files); one without relies on materials the game loads by itself, as it did before it was moved here.",
